+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+#   github-tools.patch (2026-11-03, extended 2026-11-10, by wave 16B and by the
+#   2026-11-10 22:30/23:00 rulings) - the seventeen GitHub tools, and FOURTEEN
+#   actions
+# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# The owner's decision of 2026-11-03: Jarvis may READ his GitHub (repositories,
+# open pull requests, issues, CI status, one file) and take three small ACTIONS
+# (comment, open an issue, re-run a workflow) - each action behind its own card.
+# The owner's decision of 2026-11-10 (Gemini ruling Q1, "Everything at once")
+# added the read-only PR cockpit and two more carded actions: one merge-commit-
+# only merge pinned to the head sha the card showed, and one fix-and-push into
+# an isolated scratch folder that pushes to the pull request's own branch.
+# Wave 16B (2026-11-10) added the eighth action, `github_fix_write`: the fix
+# tool's "write" stage, which changes ONE file inside that same isolated copy and
+# makes ONE commit there, with no token and no socket.
+# The 2026-11-10 22:30 ruling's Step 2 (full parity with DeepSeek) added five
+# more carded writes - `github_open_pr`, `github_workflow`,
+# `github_issue_labels`, `github_edit_pr` and `github_pr_review` - and the full
+# pull-request read `github_pr_detail`, which is decided under `github_inspect`
+# like the other reads. The absolute bans that
+# remain: force-push, squash-merge, rebase-merge, admin bypass, auto-merge,
+# bypassing required checks, pushing directly to main/master/release/*, and
+# deleting or closing a repository or a branch; a formal APPROVE review, closing
+# an issue, editing a comment, dispatching a workflow and gists stay permanently
+# banned too.
+#
+# WHY IT SITS HERE, IN THE MIDDLE OF THE FILE, AND NOT AT THE END OF IT: two
+# measured reasons, and the second is a break this patch caused before it moved.
+#
+#   * The end of this file is `web-search-grant.patch`'s own block, and its last
+#     hunk ends at EOF. `git apply -R` needs that hunk's whole post-image (three
+#     context lines plus the block) to be contiguous AND to end at the last byte
+#     of the file, so anything inserted after those three context lines makes
+#     the reversal fail - which is what happened when this patch first appended
+#     its block after it (backend/test_web_search_grant.py caught it), and the
+#     published base could no longer be reversed and re-applied byte for byte.
+#   * `unknown-risk-approvable.patch` has to stay the LAST writer of
+#     jarvis_gate.py so its own reversal cannot be disturbed
+#     (backend/test_gate_unknown_risk.py enforces it, with no whitelist), so a
+#     new gate patch must sit before it. This one sits immediately after
+#     `web-search-grant.patch`.
+#
+# It anchors on the owner's own text - the end of `review()` and the opening of
+# the `if __name__` block, which no patch writes - so backend/_stack.py's walk
+# materialises those five lines instead of reading them. That is the honest case
+# its RATCHET records, and `no-expiry-cards.patch`, `no-deadline-cards.patch` and
+# `unknown-risk-approvable.patch` pay exactly the same one.
+#
+# Both `.update` calls run at import, before any `check()`.
+#
+# WHY NOT REUSE `github_read`: that action is the scheduled look a "tell me
+# when" GitHub watch makes, and it is `"auto"` in the shipped settings so a look
+# every ten minutes is not a card every ten minutes. These tools are looks ON
+# DEMAND, at a moment the model chose, each one sending the owner's GitHub
+# token out - so they are their own action, `github_inspect`, at `"ask"`. They
+# are also in `jarvis_agent.NEEDS_A_PERSON`, so a loosened tier still asks.
+_TOOL_ACTIONS.update({
+    # `action_for_tool` needs these, or every one of the ten answers
+    # "unclassified_tool": fail-safe (that asks too), but the card would not be
+    # the card the tool raises, and the Reach page would call it unclassified.
+    "github_list": "github_inspect",
+    "github_read_file": "github_inspect",
+    "github_ci_status": "github_inspect",
+    # The read-only PR cockpit (2026-11-10) is a READ, so it is decided under the
+    # same read action as the other three - never under the merge it suggests.
+    "github_pr_scan": "github_inspect",
+    # The full pull-request read of the 22:30 ruling's Step 2 (2026-11-10): five
+    # fixed GETs, so a read, decided under the same read action.
+    "github_pr_detail": "github_inspect",
+    "github_comment": "github_comment",
+    "github_open_issue": "github_open_issue",
+    "github_rerun_workflow": "github_rerun_workflow",
+    # The one CREATE (2026-11-09, the owner's "create a repository called
+    # Fitness Band"): one new, empty repository on his own account. Its own
+    # action, so its card says "create a repository" rather than reading as
+    # "open an issue".
+    "github_create_repo": "github_create_repo",
+    # The two carded writes of 2026-11-10. Each is its own action: a card that
+    # read "read your GitHub" for a merge would be a lie, and the tier that
+    # governs a merge has to be the line the owner can find.
+    "github_merge_pr": "github_merge_pr",
+    "github_fix_and_push": "github_fix_and_push",
+    # Wave 16B (2026-11-10): the fix tool's own "write" stage, decided under its
+    # OWN action so its card says "write one file in the scratch copy" rather
+    # than "push a fix". The name passed to the gate here is an ACTION name, not
